    {'file': 'part-8-evaluation-production/module-31-production-engineering/section-31.4.html', 'svg_idx': 2, 'caption': 'Figure 31.4.3: The data flywheel turns production usage into training data, creating a self-improving cycle.', 'svg_text': 'User Interactions Feedback + Logs Curated Eval Data Improved Model', 'svg_start': 24303, 'svg_end': 26647},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.1.html', 'svg_idx': 0, 'caption': 'Figure 32.1.1 The OWASP Top 10 for LLM applications organized into three threat families, each with corresponding defensive strategies.', 'svg_text': 'Input Manipulation LLM01: Prompt Injection Direct + indirect manipulation LLM04: Model DoS Resource exhaustion via queries LLM09: Overreliance Trusting outputs blindly Defense: Input validation + rate limiting + human oversight Data &amp; Model Exploitation LLM03: Data Poisoning Corrupted training d', 'svg_start': 6842, 'svg_end': 13436},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.1.html', 'svg_idx': 1, 'caption': 'Figure 32.1.2: Four layers of defense protect LLM applications from security threats at every stage of the request lifecycle.', 'svg_text': 'Layer 1: Input Validation (regex, blocklist, Prompt Guard) Layer 2: Prompt Hardening (sandwich, delimiters, instructions) Layer 3: Output Scanning (toxicity, PII, code execution) Layer 4: Monitoring &amp; Alerting (anomaly detection, audit logs)', 'svg_start': 23565, 'svg_end': 25360},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.2.html', 'svg_idx': 0, 'caption': 'Figure 32.2.1: Three complementary approaches detect different types of hallucination at different stages.', 'svg_text': 'Self-Consistency Sample N responses at high temperature Check agreement across samples Low agreement = likely hallucination Citation Verification Extract cited claims from LLM output Verify each claim against source docs Unsupported claims = extrinsic hallucination NLI-Based Use NLI model to check e', 'svg_start': 12479, 'svg_end': 15544},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.2.html', 'svg_idx': 1, 'caption': 'Figure 32.2.2: Mitigation strategies range from RAG grounding (simple but partial) to calibrated abstention (most reliable for high-stakes use cases).', 'svg_text': 'RAG Grounding Anchor to source docs Constrained Gen JSON schema, regex Confidence Cal. Score + threshold Abstention "I don\'t know" Lower effort, partial coverage Higher effort, better coverage', 'svg_start': 18354, 'svg_end': 20808},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.2.html', 'svg_idx': 2, 'caption': 'Figure 32.2.3 A production hallucination pipeline routes LLM output through detection checks and selects the response strategy based on confidence score and application risk tolerance.', 'svg_text': 'LLM Output Raw generation Detection Battery Self-consistency check NLI entailment scoring Citation verification Confidence Score: 0.0 to 1.0 Risk Level? High conf Serve with citations Medium Serve with Caveat "Based on available info..." Low conf Abstain or Escalate "I cannot answer confidently" Log', 'svg_start': 21322, 'svg_end': 26867},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.3.html', 'svg_idx': 0, 'caption': 'Figure 32.3.1: Bias enters at every stage: data collection, training, alignment, and deployment context.', 'svg_text': 'Training Data Web crawl biases Representation gaps Pre-training Pattern amplification Frequency bias RLHF / Alignment Annotator values Cultural norms Deployment Prompt design User population', 'svg_start': 4529, 'svg_end': 7412},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.3.html', 'svg_idx': 1, 'caption': 'Figure 32.3.2 Three complementary documentation standards (model card, datasheet, system card) cover progressively broader scope from the model itself to the full deployed application.', 'svg_text': 'Model Card Covers the MODEL Intended use cases Performance metrics Ethical considerations Known limitations Bias evaluation results Audience: Users, regulators Datasheet Covers the DATA Collection methodology Demographic composition Preprocessing steps Known gaps and biases Maintenance plan Audience', 'svg_start': 20550, 'svg_end': 27061},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.3.html', 'svg_idx': 2, 'caption': "Figure 32.3.3: Environmental impact comes from both training (one-time) and inference (ongoing); inference often dominates over a model's lifetime.", 'svg_text': 'Training GPU hours: 10K-1M+ CO2: 100-10,000+ tons Water: cooling systems One-time, amortized Inference Per-query energy cost Scales with usage Often > training total Ongoing, cumulative Mitigation Smaller models (distill) Quantization (4-bit) Efficient architectures Green data centers', 'svg_start': 27638, 'svg_end': 30171},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.4.html', 'svg_idx': 0, 'caption': 'Figure 32.4.1: The EU AI Act classifies AI systems into four risk tiers with escalating compliance requirements.', 'svg_text': 'Prohibited Social scoring, manipulation High Risk Employment, credit, education, law enforcement Conformity assessment, logging, human oversight Limited Risk Chatbots, deepfakes, emotion recognition Transparency obligations only Minimal Risk (no specific obligations)', 'svg_start': 4545, 'svg_end': 6835},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.4.html', 'svg_idx': 1, 'caption': 'Figure 32.4.2 Sector-specific regulations layer on top of general AI regulations, creating compound compliance obligations for LLM deployments in healthcare, finance, and education.', 'svg_text': 'Healthcare HIPAA FDA AI/ML Guidance 21 CFR Part 11 Key requirements: PHI de-identification BAA with API providers Clinical validation Full audit trails EU AI Act: HIGH RISK Financial Services SR 11-7 (Fed Reserve) ECOA / FCRA SEC AI Guidance Key requirements: Model risk documentation Fair lending te', 'svg_start': 20175, 'svg_end': 26562},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.4.html', 'svg_idx': 2, 'caption': 'Figure 32.4.3: Regulatory approaches vary significantly by jurisdiction, from binding EU law to voluntary US frameworks.', 'svg_text': 'EU AI Act (binding law) Risk-based tiers GPAI model obligations Strictest approach US Executive orders Sector-specific rules Voluntary commitments Lighter, evolving Others UK: pro-innovation China: algorithm registry Canada: AIDA (proposed) Varied approaches', 'svg_start': 27047, 'svg_end': 29515},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.5.html', 'svg_idx': 0, 'caption': 'Figure 32.5.1: The NIST AI RMF defines four core functions; Govern is the overarching function while Map, Measure, and Manage form a continuous cycle.', 'svg_text': 'GOVERN MAP Context, stakeholders, risks MEASURE Metrics, testing, monitoring MANAGE Mitigate, respond, improve GOVERN provides overarching structure; MAP, MEASURE, MANAGE operate continuously.', 'svg_start': 4162, 'svg_end': 7002},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.5.html', 'svg_idx': 1, 'caption': 'Figure 32.5.2 A hash-chained audit trail links each LLM interaction record to its predecessor, making any modification detectable through chain verification.', 'svg_text': 'Entry #1 request_id: req_001 timestamp: 2027-03-15T10:30 model: gpt-4o user_id: u_abc123 input_hash: a3f8... output_hash: 7b2c... prev_hash: (genesis) SHA-256: 9e4d1f... Entry #2 request_id: req_002 timestamp: 2027-03-15T10:31 model: gpt-4o user_id: u_def456 input_hash: c1d9... output_hash: 5e8f... ', 'svg_start': 13493, 'svg_end': 20586},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.5.html', 'svg_idx': 2, 'caption': "Figure 32.5.3: SR 11-7's three lines of defense separate model development, independent validation, and audit oversight.", 'svg_text': '1st Line Model Development Business units own risk ML engineers, product teams, data scientists 2nd Line Model Validation Independent challenge Risk management, compliance teams 3rd Line Internal Audit Framework effectiveness Internal/external audit functions', 'svg_start': 21086, 'svg_end': 23555},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.6.html', 'svg_idx': 0, 'caption': 'Figure 32.6.1: Model licenses range from fully open (Apache 2.0) to proprietary API-only access with no weight availability.', 'svg_text': 'Apache 2.0 Most open Llama License Open weights + AUP CC-BY-NC Non-commercial only Proprietary API No weights access', 'svg_start': 4801, 'svg_end': 6686},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.6.html', 'svg_idx': 1, 'caption': 'Figure 32.6.2 DP-SGD protects training data privacy through gradient clipping (bounding individual influence) and calibrated noise injection, with a privacy budget (epsilon) tracking cumulative privac', 'svg_text': 'Step 1: Per-Sample Gradients g1 g2 (large) g3 g4 (medium) clip norm C clip Step 2: Clip to Max Norm g1 g2 clipped g3 g4 clipped max norm C avg Step 3: Average + Noise avg + noise = update noise ~ N(0, sigma * C / batch_size) Privacy Guarantee: Why This Works Gradient Clipping Bounds the maximum infl', 'svg_start': 13216, 'svg_end': 21446},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.6.html', 'svg_idx': 2, 'caption': 'Figure 32.6.3: IP ownership questions span training data rights, output copyrightability, and fine-tuned model status.', 'svg_text': 'Training Data Fair use for training? Opt-out mechanisms? Compensation models? Active litigation Model Outputs Who owns AI text? Copyrightable? User vs. provider? Jurisdiction-dependent Fine-Tuned Models Derivative work? License inheritance? Trade secret status? License-dependent', 'svg_start': 22110, 'svg_end': 24644},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.7.html', 'svg_idx': 0, 'caption': 'Figure 32.7.1: Unlearning methods trade off between forgetting guarantees and computational cost.', 'svg_text': 'Exact Unlearning Retrain from scratch on filtered dataset Guarantee: complete Cost: prohibitive for large LLMs Gold standard Approximate Gradient ascent on forget set Guarantee: partial Cost: moderate (few epochs) Most practical Weight Editing Task vectors, LOKA, representation surgery Guarantee: ta', 'svg_start': 5211, 'svg_end': 8569},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.7.html', 'svg_idx': 1, 'caption': "Figure 32.7.2 Two geometric views of unlearning in weight space. Gradient ascent (left) moves away from the forget set's loss minimum. Task vector negation (right) subtracts the learned skill directio", 'svg_text': 'Gradient Ascent Unlearning w2 w1 Original Model Gradient Descent (normal training) Gradient ASCENT (maximize loss on forget set) Retain loss pulls back (preserve general ability) Unlearned Model Forget set loss contours Task Vector Unlearning w2 w1 Base Model Fine-tuned (has target skill) Task Vecto', 'svg_start': 15288, 'svg_end': 21786},
    {'file': 'part-9-safety-strategy/module-32-safety-ethics-regulation/section-32.7.html', 'svg_idx': 2, 'caption': 'Figure 32.7.3: Good unlearning must score well on all three axes: forgetting the target data, retaining general capability, and resisting membership inference attacks.', 'svg_text': 'Forget Quality Can the model still reproduce the data? Metric: accuracy on forget set (lower = better) Target: near random Retain Quality Does the model still work on other tasks? Metric: accuracy on retain set (higher = better) Target: unchanged Privacy Can an attacker detect the removed data? Metr', 'svg_start': 24191, 'svg_end': 27001},
